ART verifies every method before running it. Hard failures make the class unusable and throw java.lang.VerifyError when it is loaded; the message names the class, the method, the offset in code units (like [0x4]) and the reason. Typical causes:
- register type mismatch, such as passing an int register where a reference is expected or using half of a wide pair;
- reading a register that was never assigned on some path;
- using an object from new-instance before its <init> constructor ran;
- a move-result that does not directly follow an invoke;
- execution that can fall off the end of the code without a return or throw;
- monitor-enter/monitor-exit that do not pair up, which can force the method to run without compiled code or be rejected.

